
       HOW TO READ AND WRITE DISKETTES


We use the 'tar' program to store most data on diskettes. tar is a convenient
way to put data (including subdirectories) into diskette. The simplest way
to use tar is:

1	You must format the diskette first, by inserting a diskette,
	and entering "diskformat /dev/rs0a". This will take a few minutes.

2	Then, to place files onto the diskette, enter:

		tar cvf /dev/rs0a <filelist> 

	where <filelist> is the files you want to put on the diskette.
	You can say tar cvf /dev/rs0a myfile, - or -
		    tar cvf /dev/rs0a .  - where "." means the current
	directory and all its subdirectories - or -
		    tar cvf /dev/rs0a /usr/company/myfile - where you
	give a fully-qualified filelist.

	Please read the tar manual pages for a fuller explanation.

	NOTE: The above examples require that all the files fit on 1 diskette,
		which holds 800 blocks maximum (400Kbytes). If you need to
		dump more than 1 diskette's worth, you can do this by
		entering tar cvfB /dev/rs0a 800 <filelist>
		and tar will eject the diskette after it is full and prompt
		you for another diskette, etc.

3	To read in a diskette you have made with tar, enter
		tar xvf /dev/rs0a  - the whole diskette is read.

	To read in a specific file on the tar diskette, enter
		tar xvf /dev/rs0a myfile - ie, give the name.

4	To get a list of what is on the diskette, enter
		tar tvf /dev/rs0a.

YOU CAN ONLY READ IN A DISKETTE WITH TAR IF YOU CREATED IT WITH TAR.

The other way to write/read on diskettes is to "mount" them and simply
write onto them. Here is a brief example. (Again the diskette must be first
formatted.)

	mount /dev/s0a /t     (notice -- it's /dev/s0a this way.)
	cp myfile /t/myfile
	.
	.
	.
	cd /t
	ls -l
	.
	.
	.

